  <section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Open research · development preview</p><h1>Test search methods before applying them to the manuscript.</h1><p class="lede">The first development study tested whether our searches could recover known Latin and Italian messages hidden with three defined encoding methods.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/research/development">Review the development results →</a><a class="text-link" href="/contribute">Preview browser participation →</a></div><p class="small muted">New assignments and account sign-in are currently closed. A fresh-case evaluation is in progress; no evaluation report is available yet.</p></div>
  <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-label="Research sequence"><div class="figure-top"><span>How the research works</span><span>01 — 03</span></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Recover a known message.</h3><p>Hide text with a defined encoding and measure whether the search recovers it.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Compare the result.</h3><p>Run the same search on shuffled and generated texts, and retain every failure.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Publish the record.</h3><p>Release the rules, unchanged output, and reproduction files for review.</p></div></div><figcaption class="figure-caption">The same declared tests apply to successful and unsuccessful searches.</figcaption></figure></section>
